{
 "name": "House — wireframe",
 "notes": [
  "A 10 × 7 m house (7 m over all, the garage's wall bands inside it) on a sloped parcel, its ground and first floors pushed 1 m out of the downhill face and the garage 3 m, the driveway cut into the hill and ramped down to the road.",
  "The house is turned 8.06° about its south-east corner to lie parallel to the north-east boundary, 1 m off it (D6).",
  "Heights are metres above the garage-floor datum: real 991.11 m a.s.l. is y 0. The parcel and relief come from the terrain app's survey.",
  "Levels in a terrain profile are finished surfaces; a slab's top is that number, its underside `slab` below; a wall is `wall` thick on the hill side of the line it holds — except the garage's own bands, which lie inside the envelope (z 0–0.3 and 6.7–7) so the house is 7 m over all.",
  "The garage is 6 m clear, x 5.7–11.7 (`x0` 5.7, `extendFront` 1.7), with a 0.30 front wall at x 11.7–12 carrying the 5.00 × 2.25 sectional-door opening under a lintel; the box sits 0.30 further into the hill than the earlier x 6–12 so the clear length and the 3.70 apron both hold. The back 3.7 m of the hill stay in place under the ground floor, so the pit is shallower there and the retaining walls behind it shorter. Inside, along the north wall: a 1.00 stair (z 0.3–1.3) with a railing on its open side, a 1.36 landing inside the door, 16 treads of 0.29 rising 17 × 0.171 to the hall at the back wall line (the 17th riser is the wall), storage under the back 2.25 of the flight, open to the garage, and 5.35 across for two slots (car 2.60 left, quad or second car 2.75 right). The ground and first floors are moved 1 m forward and shortened 1 m at the back, x 2–11, 9 m long (`x0` 2, `extendFront` 1; the first floor ends at x 6.7, see below), so the ground floor's slab covers the garage to x 11. A 2 m balcony at ground-floor level (`balcony`, x 11–13) stands in front of the ground floor: its first metre is the garage's roof, its second projects over the garage door. The envelope stays x 0–10: it is the frame the turn and the cut are measured from, not the house's outline.",
  "The apron is what the cut leaves in front of the garage door, so it carries its own `ring` (model frame): the fillet's corner (13, 7), the outer wall, the cut's north edge back to the house face (8.87, −1), the face down to the house's north-east corner (9.02, 0.07), the garage's north edge to its corner (11, −0.21) and the garage's turned face to its south-east corner (11.98, 6.72). Recompute those four turned points if the turn or the garage's depth changes.",
  "The yard south of the house is set out on the house's own frame, turned with it: its north edge is the house's south wall (house x 2–11 at z 7), its west and east edges run in line with the house's end walls (house x 2 and x 11) down to the parcel boundary, and the boundary closes it. The east edge reaches the boundary at x 12.54, inside the ramp's uphill wall at 12.7. It is cut down to the ground floor's 0 where the hill stands above it; where the ground lies below, no fill is planned yet — that is decided once the house is built (`fill: true` and a `taper` band on the east edge would add it). Recompute the ring (the four turned points) if the turn or the floors' extent changes.",
  "The ground floor (x 2–11, z 0–7, its 0.30 outer walls inside that, so 8.40 × 6.40 clear) is planned around where the garage flight lands: its top riser is the garage's back wall line, x 5.7, so you step out at the back of the north strip facing the hill, in the floor's darkest corner. That corner is the hall — the landing (x 2.3–5.7, z 0.3–1.5) and a lobby south of it (x 4.7–6.7, z 1.5–3.4) — with the toilet (x 3.4–4.6, z 1.6–3.4, door on its east wall) and the bedroom door (z 3.4, x 4.8–5.7) off it and the living room open to its east. The stair to the first floor is one straight flight along the back wall, x 2.3–3.3, from the hall's west end climbing south: 17 risers of 2.90/17 on 0.27 treads, foot at z 1.4, top riser at z 5.72, a 0.98 landing to the south wall at the top; a 0.10 wall on its east side is the toilet's and the bedroom's west wall, and the bedroom's closet is under its upper half (door at z 5.5–6.4). The first floor's opening for it (`holes`) is x 2.3–3.3, z 2.48–5.72, from the fifth riser, with rails on its east edge and north end. The bedroom takes the south-west corner (x 3.4–6.6, z 3.5–6.7, a 1.40 window to the yard). The living room with the kitchen is the whole front, x 6.7–10.7, z 1.5–6.7 plus the nook by the garage opening (x 9.18–10.7, z 0.3–1.5; the opening, x 5.7–9.18 × z 0.3–1.5 in the ground floor's deck, is railed on its south edge and east end), and it is tall: the first floor stops at x 6.7 (its `x1`, on the bedroom wall, a gallery rail on its edge), so the room rises past the first floor to the roof. The roof is a gable with its ridge along the house over z 3.5: 45°, eaves at 5.60 on the north and south walls, ridge at 9.10, 0.40 of overhang, two `works` slopes whose `y0End`/`y1End` carry the pitch. The panorama is the east wall itself, the living room's alone: one pentagon of glass between the north and south walls, 6.40 wide, 5.90 at the walls and 9.10 at the apex, with a 1.00 door to the balcony at its south end; the entrance from the yard is a 0.90 door in the south wall beside the kitchen, and a 1.40 window further east. The kitchen runs along the bedroom wall (x 6.7–7.3). Partitions are 0.10. The study is `ground-floor-study.html`." ],
